+### 8/30/26, 1:55 PM — JD Smith (OP)
+
+Got it, thanks. So if something is (1) Biblical and (2) Traditional, does it still need (4) and (5) (allowed by bishop & rector) to be legitimate? Or are those there for cases where the first levels don't settle it on their own?
+
+---
+
+### 8/30/26, 3:05 PM — Athanasius325 / Fr James
+
+In very practical terms, it would need to be allowed by the Bishop and the Rector for it to be a part of public worship in any particular parish. If the first three demand a particular practice (take an easy example, like the reading of Scripture as part of public worship) and the Bishop and/or Rector level rejects that, then he/they is/are in rebellion and should be disciplined. 
+
+If the first three levels allow for a practice but do not demand it (incense, for instance), then the Bishop and then the Rector decide the practice. The Bishop typically will simply allow for the option of incense, and the Rector decides what the practice will be (along the lines of those higher authorities, of course).
+
+---
+
+### 8/30/26, 4:01 PM — JD Smith (OP)
+
+@Athanasius325 / Fr James So based on what you're saying, incense is permitted by the first three levels but not required by them. Meaning a parish that never used incense at all wouldn't be failing at levels 1 through 3 the way they would if they never read Scripture?
+
+---
+
+### 8/30/26, 4:12 PM — Athanasius325 / Fr James
+
+Correct. Now, if you're talking about St Francis Anglican Church, for us it would go all the way to the 4th on allowed, and the 5th (me, as the Rector) establishes it as part of normative practice for Holy Communion.
+
+---
+
